Q: Are there any nice ways to split Crypto++ Integer in Two? A: <p><a href="https://github.com/weidai11/cryptopp/blob/master/integer.h" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>CryptoCPP::Integer</code></a> supports <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bitwise_operation" rel="nofollow noreferrer">bitwise operations</a>, similarly to built in integral types (<code>int</code> etc.).</p>
<p>Therefore you can do the following:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>CryptoPP::Integer rMask(&quot;0x00000000000000000000000000000000ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff&quot;);

CryptoPP::Integer xL(x &gt;&gt; 128);
CryptoPP::Integer xR(x &amp; rMask);
</code></pre>
<p>(<code>xL</code> and <code>xR</code> will now hold the left and right half parts of <code>x</code>).</p>
<hr />
<p>Another approach, suggested in comments by @n.m.is-an-unemployed-ai-agent is to use <code>Integer::DivideByPowerOf2</code>. This API enables to get the quotient as well as remainder of a division by a power of 2. If you use 128 as your power of 2, these will be the 2 parts of the number that you are looking for (where the right part is the remainder):</p>
<pre><code>CryptoPP::Integer xL;
CryptoPP::Integer xR;
Integer::DivideByPowerOf2(xR, xL, x, 128);
</code></pre>
<p>This approach might also be more performant (but this requires validation via measurements).</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Update:</strong><br />
The OP confirmed in comments (<a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79995532/are-there-any-nice-ways-to-split-crypto-integer-in-two?noredirect=1#comment141141613_79995532">here</a> and <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79995532/are-there-any-nice-ways-to-split-crypto-integer-in-two#comment141141614_79995541">here</a>) that based on performance testing using <code>DivideByPowerOf2</code> (the 2nd approach) is indeed the fastest.</p>

Q: SwiftUI HStack with Text and Picker alignment and spacing A: <p>You have chosen to use <code>.navigationLink</code> as the picker style. By default, it seems that this style expands to use as much width as possible. However, the width can be reduced to the minimum necessary by applying <code>.fixedSize()</code>. Then:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Add aditional padding after applying <code>.fixedSize</code> and before applying the border, as needed. Unfortunately, it seems that the picker style <code>.navigationLink</code> always shows the picker label, even when <code>.labelsHidden()</code> is applied. For the first picker, you are using an empty string as the label, but a gap between the empty string and the picker selection is still present. So to make the horizontal padding look balanced, only apply trailing padding, leading padding is not needed.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Apply the border after the padding. As mentioned in a comment, you might want to consider using an overlay to draw the border around the picker, instead of using <code>.border</code> + <code>.cornerRadius</code>. My answer to <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/77757371/20386264">How to add a border in SwiftUI with clipShape?</a> shows how this can be done.</p>
</li>
<li><p>After applying the border, apply a frame to increase the width of the picker to the maximum available, with leading alignment. This way, the size of the picker remains constant and the picker selection appears immediately after the text label. If the same technique is used for the second picker too, the surplus space is shared between them.</p>
</li>
<li><p>If you don't want the text labels to wrap then <code>.fixedSize()</code> can be applied to these too.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Since a blank label is being used for the pickers, use <code>.accessibilityLabel</code> to add a meaningful label for the benefit of Voice Over.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Since you are using the same styling twice, you might like to encapsulate the styling in a wrapper view. Something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>struct BorderedPicker&lt;T&gt;: View
where T: Hashable, T: Identifiable, T: RawRepresentable, T.RawValue: StringProtocol {
    let label: String
    @Binding var selection: T
    let allCases: [T]

    var body: some View {
        HStack {
            Text(label)
                .fixedSize()
                .accessibilityHidden(true)

            Picker(&quot;&quot;, selection: $selection) {
                ForEach(allCases) { val in
                    Text(val.rawValue)
                }
            }
            .accessibilityLabel(label)
            .pickerStyle(.navigationLink)
            .foregroundStyle(.primary)
            .fixedSize()
            .padding(.trailing, 8)
            .padding(.vertical, 5)
            .overlay {
                RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 5)
                    .stroke(.gray, lineWidth: 2)
            }
            .frame(maxWidth: .infinity, alignment: .leading)
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><code>BorderedPicker</code> can now be plugged into your code. Here is how the snippets can be turned into a standalone example:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>struct ContentView: View {

    enum Countries: String, CaseIterable, Identifiable {
        case UnitedStates = &quot;USA&quot;, France, UK = &quot;England&quot;, Canada
        var id: Countries { self }
    }
    @State private var selectedCountry: Countries = .UnitedStates

    enum States: String, CaseIterable, Identifiable {
        case Alaska, Alabama, Nebraska, NY = &quot;New York&quot;
        var id: States { self }
    }
    @State private var selectedState: States = .NY

    var body: some View {
        NavigationStack {
            HStack {

                // Country picker
                BorderedPicker(
                    label: &quot;Country&quot;,
                    selection: $selectedCountry,
                    allCases: Countries.allCases
                )
                .onChange(of: selectedCountry) {}

                // State/Province picker
                BorderedPicker(
                    label: &quot;State/Province&quot;,
                    selection: $selectedState,
                    allCases: States.allCases
                )
                .onChange(of: selectedState) {}
            }
            .padding(.horizontal)
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
