Q: How can I detect back navigation on the client? A: <p>I know this is a very old question but I hit a similar issue in that my server was taking so long to produce the next page (lots of SQL queries on big data) that I had to pop up a &quot;Please wait&quot; modal when they submitted the form. That modal was still showing if they hit back on the new page hence me hunting around and finding this thread - however, the proposed solutions all felt a little too involved for me so I hunted and found a little more straightforward one: When the server starts sending the new page, the current page (with the Please wait modal) gets a &quot;beforeunload&quot; event so I simply applied the following code to tidy up the page 3 seconds later giving the next page plenty of time to load before my modal closes:</p>
<pre><code>window.onbeforeunload = function()
{
    setTimeout( function() {
        // do stuff to prepare the page in case back button is hit
        // in my case close my pleasewait modal
        $('#pleasewait').hide();
        // but you could equally re-enable the submit button etc
    }, 3000);
}
</code></pre>
<p>In case you are wondering, certainly on Chrome: the page with this code on does not sit there displayed for 3 seconds before the new page is rendered - if the new page renders instantly then the origin page disappears immediately but the please wait modal is closed if you hit back still. You could do much less than 3 seconds I am sure but hey, it worked for me :)</p>
